            "number": 1638,
            "title": "fix(query): validate media type tokens in requireContentType",
            "body": "Resolves #1633\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n- **Media Type Token Validation:** Per [RFC 9110 §8.3.1 and §5.6.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-media-type), a media type consists of `<type>/<subtype>` where `type` and `subtype` must each be a valid HTTP `token` (`1*tchar`).\n  - Previously, `requireContentType` only verified `slash <= 0 || slash === mediaType.length - 1`, allowing malformed Content-Type values with multiple slashes (e.g. `application/json/extra`), whitespace (e.g. `application/json junk`), or delimiters (e.g. `application/(json)`) to erroneously match when wildcard acceptance (e.g. `application/*`) was used.\n  - Replaced the simple slash index check with `MEDIA_TYPE_RE = /^[!#$%&'*+\\-.^_`|~0-9a-zA-Z]+\\/[!#$%&'*+\\-.^_`|~0-9a-zA-Z]+$/`, ensuring malformed media types consistently throw `422 Unprocessable Content` regardless of whether exact or wildcard matching is configured.\n- **Tests:**\n  - Added unit test in `test/query.test.ts` verifying that extra slashes, embedded whitespace, and invalid delimiter characters reject with 422 under wildcard acceptance.\n  - All 3,120 tests pass across the entire suite.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n<!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Malformed `Content-Type` values are now rejected with a 422 response, including values with extra slashes, trailing characters, or invalid punctuation.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
